\ curry ( value xt -- xt' ): bind a value into a new anonymous word — xt'
\ pushes the value, then calls xt. The bound value is captured at curry time;
\ the curried xt travels through other words' frames intact.

\ === basic partial application ===
5 ' + curry constant add-five
10 add-five execute . cr             \ 15
3 add-five execute . cr              \ 8

clear

\ === any value kind binds: floats, arrays, xts ===
[ 1 2 3 ] 2 ' take curry execute .a cr                       \ [ 1 2 ]
' sqrt ' execute curry constant apply-sqrt
16 apply-sqrt execute . cr                                   \ 4

clear

\ === a curried quotation survives another word's locals frame ===
: mapper-word | items quotation | items quotation map ; internal
[ 4 9 16 ] ' sqrt [: element xt | element xt execute :] curry mapper-word .a cr  \ [ 2 3 4 ]

clear

\ === capture is by value at curry time ===
variable bound-source
7 to bound-source
bound-source ' negate curry constant negate-seven
9 to bound-source
negate-seven execute . cr            \ -7 (rebinding the variable after curry changes nothing)

clear

\ === 2curry ( a b xt -- xt' ): bind two values into one word — xt' pushes a,
\ then b, then calls xt (one word and one call, versus curry curry) ===
10 3 ' - 2curry execute . cr          \ 7   (order preserved: 10 3 -, not 3 10 -)
10 3 ' - curry curry execute . cr     \ 7   (same result as chained curry)

: weighted-sum | a b | a 2 * b 3 * + ; internal
4 5 ' weighted-sum 2curry execute . cr        \ 23  (both reach the target: 4*2 + 5*3)

clear

\ === a curried token reports its bound values, then the target renders as
\ itself: the values are data in the token, not literals compiled into a body.
\ has? is a regex match, so the parens and brackets are escaped ===
5 [: + :] curry see-compiled>string "curried, binds 5" has? . cr   \ 1
5 [: + :] curry see-compiled>string "0: \+" has? . cr              \ 1
5 ' negate curry see>string "curried, binds 5" has? . cr           \ 1
5 ' negate curry see>string "negate is a primitive" has? . cr      \ 1

clear

\ === a token is a value: it prints as the target plus its bound count, and
\ type-of calls it an xt, the same as a plain one ===
5 ' + curry .                        \ <xt + +1 bound>
cr
5 ' + curry type-of .                \ :xt
' + type-of .                        \ :xt
cr

clear

\ === every combinator takes one ===
[ 1 2 3 ] 10 ' + curry map .a cr                     \ [ 11 12 13 ]
[ 1 2 3 4 ] 2 ' > curry filter .a cr                 \ [ 3 4 ] — element > the bound 2
[ 1 2 3 ] 0 2 [: * + :] curry reduce . cr            \ 12 — accumulator + element * the bound 2
[ 5 6 7 ] 6 ' eq curry find-first . cr               \ 6
[ 1 2 ] [ 10 20 ] 100 [: + + :] curry 2 nmap .a cr   \ [ 111 122 ] — the bound value lands above both rows
7 ' + curry constant add-seven
0 [: add-seven execute :] 3 times . cr               \ 21
[ 3 1 2 ] -1 ' * curry sort-by .a cr                 \ [ 3 2 1 ] — keyed by element * -1

clear

\ === a token works inside a parallel region, and may be built in one ===
[ 1 2 3 4 ] 10 ' + curry pmap .a cr                  \ [ 11 12 13 14 ]
[ 1 2 3 4 ] [: 100 ' + curry execute :] pmap .a cr   \ [ 101 102 103 104 ]
[ 1 2 3 4 ] 1 ' > curry pfilter .a cr                \ [ 2 3 4 ] — element > the bound 1

clear

\ === tokens are collected: currying per call has no ceiling ===
: bind-once  1 ' + curry drop ;
[: bind-once :] 50000 times  gc  "50k curries" . cr  \ 50k curries

\ === a token survives collection while held in a global and in an array ===
3 ' + curry constant add-three
[ 4 ' * curry ] constant held
gc
10 add-three execute . cr            \ 13
5 held 0 @i execute . cr             \ 20

clear

\ === update-at accepts one ===
{ :n 5 } dup :n 10 ' + curry update-at :n @ . cr     \ 15

clear

\ === the token is rooted for the whole combinator call: collecting inside the
\ body cannot free it, and currying a token flattens — the outer binding
\ pushes first, so order matches curry curry ===
[ 1 2 3 ] 10 [: gc + :] curry map .a cr              \ [ 11 12 13 ]
1 [: + :] curry 2 swap curry execute . cr            \ 3 — pushes 2, then 1, then +
5 ' + curry 0 ncurry constant zero-bound
10 zero-bound execute . cr                           \ 15 — binding nothing keeps the target's own bindings

clear

\ === a token survives a binary image round trip, held in a global and in an
\ array; the reloaded token still executes ===
6 ' + curry constant add-six
[ 3 ' * curry ] constant held-in-array
"/tmp/lf_curried.img" save-image
"/tmp/lf_curried.img" load-image
gc
4 add-six execute . cr                               \ 10
5 held-in-array 0 @i execute . cr                    \ 15

clear

\ === errors ===
[: 1 2 curry :] [: . cr :] try-catch          \ (top is not an xt)
[: 5 curry :] [: . cr :] try-catch            \ (needs two operands)
[: 1 2 3 2curry :] [: . cr :] try-catch       \ (top is not an xt)
[: 1 2 2curry :] [: . cr :] try-catch         \ (needs three operands)
